\title[Rational star pencils of the Berggren tree]{The Rational Star Pencils of the Berggren Tree\\
\normalsize Charge, hypercycles, quantisation, Farey visibility, and a totient density law
for the Pythagorean tree in the Poincar\'e half-plane}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Plotting the Berggren ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples in the Poincar\'e upper
half-plane through the Euclid embedding $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$ produces a star map: pencils of
straight rays fanning out of points on the ideal boundary. The two conspicuous fans sit at
the ideal points $0$ and $1$, but fainter fans are visible at $1/2$, $1/3$, $1/5$, $2/5$ and
beyond. We give a complete and exact account of this phenomenon. To each rational ideal
point $p/q$ in lowest terms and each integral pair $(m,n)$ we attach the \emph{charge}
$\chg{p/q}(m,n)=pm-qn$, and prove: the nodes of a fixed charge lie on a single Euclidean ray
emanating from $p/q$; that ray is a hypercycle, at hyperbolic distance exactly
$\arsinh(|\chi|/q)$ from the complete geodesic over $p/q$; the set of charges realised by
Euclid seeds is all of $\ZZ$ when $p+q$ is odd and exactly the odd integers when $p+q$ is
even, with every realised ray infinite; the axis of a fan carries at most one node, namely
$(q,p)$, and does so precisely when $p+q$ is odd; adjacent rays are separated by exactly
$y/q$ at plot height $y$, so the fans resolved at resolution $\varepsilon$ are the Farey
fractions of level $\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$, of cardinality $\sum_{q\le
Q}\varphi(q)$; the charge measures Diophantine approximation, the innermost rays consisting
of Farey neighbours of $p/q$, and every node with $m\ge2$ is an innermost node of two
distinct fans of smaller denominator; a ray of odd charge $k$ at an odd/odd rational carries
exactly $2\varphi(|k|)$ nodes per window of $2|k|$ parameters, hence has arithmetic density
$\varphi(|k|)/|k|$; and the three tree moves act on the fan parameter $(p,q)$ by an integral
linear transport that conserves the charge exactly, preserves primitivity, and preserves the
parity of $p+q$, whence the $0$-fan and the $1$-fan lie in permanently distinct classes.
\end{abstract}

