\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The picture}

Three objects meet in this paper.

\emph{Primitive Pythagorean triples.} A triple $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with
$a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$. Euclid's parametrisation writes each of them uniquely
(with the even leg listed second) as
\[
(a,b,c)=(m^2-n^2,\ 2mn,\ m^2+n^2)
\]
for a \emph{Euclid seed} $(m,n)$: a pair of positive integers with $0<n<m$, $\gcd(m,n)=1$
and $m+n$ odd.

\emph{The Berggren tree.} In seed coordinates the three classical Berggren matrices become
the three affine moves
\[
B_1(m,n)=(2m-n,\ m),\qquad B_2(m,n)=(2m+n,\ m),\qquad B_3(m,n)=(m+2n,\ n),
\]
each of which maps Euclid seeds to Euclid seeds, and which generate every Euclid seed from
the root $(2,1)$ exactly once. Preservation of seedhood is immediate: positivity and $n'<m'$
follow from $0<n<m$; a common divisor of $2m-n$ and $m$ divides $n$; and each move changes
the coordinate sum by an even amount.

\emph{The Poincar\'e half-plane.} $\HH=\{z\in\CC:\Ima z>0\}$ with $ds=|dz|/\Ima z$, ideal
boundary $\RR\cup\{\infty\}$, geodesics the vertical rays and the semicircles orthogonal to
$\RR$, and
\[
\cosh d(z,w)=1+\frac{|z-w|^2}{2\,\Ima z\,\Ima w}.
\]

The bridge is the \emph{Euclid embedding}
\[
z(m,n)=\frac{n+i}{m}=\frac nm+\frac im,
\]
injective on seeds, since the height recovers $m$ and the real part then recovers $n$.
Rendering the node set produces a picture with three unmistakable features: nested arcs,
one distinguished ray marching off at constant speed, and---the subject of this
paper---pencils of straight rays fanning out of boundary points, obvious at $0$ and $1$ and
visible at $0.5$, $0.333\ldots$, $0.2$ and elsewhere.

\subsection{Background: the radial coordinate}

For orientation we record the identity governing the radial structure of the picture.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:radial}
For every $m\ge1$ and $n\ge0$,
\[
\cosh d_{\HH}\bigl(i,\ z(m,n)\bigr)=\frac{m^2+n^2+1}{2m}=\frac{c+1}{2m},\qquad c=m^2+n^2 .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
With $z=i$ and $w=(n+i)/m$ one has $\Ima w=1/m$ and $|z-w|^2=(n^2+(m-1)^2)/m^2$;
substituting into the distance formula and simplifying gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:radialbound}
$\tfrac12\log c\le d_{\HH}(i,z(m,n))\le\tfrac12\log\bigl(2(c+1)\bigr)$, so
$|d-\tfrac12\log c|\le\log2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\cosh(\tfrac12\log c)=(c+1)/(2\sqrt c)$, so the lower bound is $m\le\sqrt c$. For the upper
bound, $\cosh d\le e^d\le2\cosh d$ gives $d\le\log((c+1)/m)$, and $n<m$ forces $2m^2\ge c+1$,
whence $(c+1)/m\le\sqrt{2(c+1)}$.
\end{proof}

Thus the hypotenuse is the radial coordinate to within $\log2$: nodes are organised into
shells by $c$. The \emph{angular} structure is the fan structure, and that is what we
determine.

\section{Charge, rays, and hypercycles}

\begin{definition}[Charge]
For integers $p,q$ and a pair $(m,n)$, the \emph{charge of $(m,n)$ at $p/q$} is
\[
\chg{p/q}(m,n)=p\,m-q\,n\in\ZZ .
\]
Throughout, $p/q$ is in lowest terms with $q\ge1$; we write $\chi$ when $p/q$ is clear.
\end{definition}

Two familiar special cases: $\chg{0/1}(m,n)=-n$ and $\chg{1/1}(m,n)=m-n$, the parameters of
the two classical stars.

\begin{theorem}[Rational star line]\label{thm:ray}
Let $q\ge1$, $m\ge1$ and $z=z(m,n)$. Then
\[
\frac pq-\Rea z=\frac{\chg{p/q}(m,n)}{q}\cdot\Ima z .
\]
Consequently all pairs with a common charge at $p/q$ lie on one and the same Euclidean ray
emanating from the ideal point $p/q$, of Euclidean slope $q/\chi$ (vertical when $\chi=0$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\dfrac pq-\dfrac nm=\dfrac{pm-qn}{qm}=\dfrac{\chi}{q}\cdot\dfrac1m$, and $\Ima z=1/m$.
\end{proof}

This single line is the whole visual phenomenon: the rays of the picture are the level sets
of the charge, and there is one such pencil at \emph{every} rational boundary point. The
classical fans at $0$ and $1$ are the case $q=1$, in which the charge is smallest and the
rays widest.

\begin{theorem}[Hypercycle theorem]\label{thm:hyper}
Let $x\in\RR$, let $\gamma_x$ be the vertical geodesic from $x$ to $\infty$, and let
$z\in\HH$ satisfy $x-\Rea z=u\,\Ima z$. Then
\[
d_{\HH}(z,\gamma_x)=\arsinh|u| ,
\]
the infimum being attained at the point of $\gamma_x$ of height $\Ima(z)\sqrt{1+u^2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $y=\Ima z$ and $w=x+is$. Then $(\Rea z-x)^2=u^2y^2$, so
\[
\cosh d(z,w)=1+\frac{u^2y^2+(y-s)^2}{2ys}=\frac{(1+u^2)y^2+s^2}{2ys}\ \ge\ \sqrt{1+u^2}
\]
by the arithmetic--geometric mean inequality, with equality iff $s=y\sqrt{1+u^2}$. Finally
$\arcosh\sqrt{1+u^2}=\arsinh|u|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The fan is a ladder of hypercycles]\label{cor:ladder}
The hyperbolic distance from $z(m,n)$ to the complete geodesic over $p/q$ is
\[
d_{\HH}\bigl(z(m,n),\gamma_{p/q}\bigr)=\arsinh\!\left(\frac{|\chg{p/q}(m,n)|}{q}\right),
\]
depending on the node only through its charge. The fan at $p/q$ is a discrete pencil of
hypercycles at the quantised levels $\arsinh(k/q)$, $k\in\ZZ$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Off-axis separation]\label{cor:offaxis}
Every node of nonzero charge at $p/q$ lies at distance at least $\arsinh(1/q)$ from the axis
$\gamma_{p/q}$, and every node of charge $|\chi|\le K$ lies within the band of width
$\arsinh(K/q)$. The lower bound is attained: the seed $(3,2)$ has charge $-1$ at $1/2$, hence
lies at distance exactly $\arsinh(1/2)$ from $\gamma_{1/2}$.
\end{corollary}

Since $q\mapsto\arsinh(1/q)$ is strictly decreasing, fans of large denominator are squeezed
into thin pencils; the quantitative form of this is the visibility law of
Section~\ref{sec:vis}.

\section{Parity quantisation and the axis}

\begin{theorem}[Parity quantisation]\label{thm:parity}
If $p$ and $q$ are both odd, then every Euclid seed has \emph{odd} charge at $p/q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Modulo $2$, $\chi=pm-qn\equiv m-n\equiv m+n\equiv1$, using $p\equiv q\equiv1$ and the seed
condition.
\end{proof}

So at $1/3$, $1/5$, $3/5$, $5/7$, \ldots\ the fan is half empty: the rays of even charge
carry no node at all. This explains the classical asymmetry between the two conspicuous
stars: at $p/q=1/1$ both entries are odd and the charge $m-n$ is always odd, while at
$p/q=0/1$ the entries have opposite parity and the charge $-n$ takes every value.

\begin{theorem}[The axis carries at most one node]\label{thm:axis}
Let $p/q$ be in lowest terms with $q\ge1$ and let $(m,n)$ be a Euclid seed. Then
$\chg{p/q}(m,n)=0$ if and only if $(m,n)=(q,p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\chi=0$ means $pm=qn$. Since $\gcd(p,q)=1$ we get $q\mid m$; since $\gcd(m,n)=1$ and
$m\mid qn$ we get $m\mid q$. Hence $m=q$, and then $qp=qn$ gives $n=p$. The converse is a
substitution.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[When the axis is occupied]\label{thm:axisparity}
For coprime $0<p<q$, there is a Euclid seed of charge $0$ at $p/q$ if and only if $p+q$ is
odd; the seed is $(q,p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:axis} the only candidate is $(q,p)$, which satisfies $0<p<q$ and
$\gcd(q,p)=1$; it is a Euclid seed exactly when $q+p$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
At $p/q=1/2$ the axis node is $(2,1)$, the root of the Berggren tree and the seed of
$(3,4,5)$: the tree's origin sits exactly on the centre line of the fan at $0.5$. At $1/3$
and $1/5$ the axis is empty---those fans have a hole down the middle.
\end{example}

\section{The unimodular parametrisation and the realisation theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} rules charges out; we now rule them in. Because $\gcd(p,q)=1$ there
are integers $a,b$ with $pb-qa=1$. Define
\[
\sigma(k,s)=\bigl(kb+sq,\ ka+sp\bigr),\qquad s\in\ZZ .
\]

\begin{proposition}[Properties of $\sigma$]\label{prop:sigma}
With $pb-qa=1$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Constant charge}: $\chg{p/q}(\sigma(k,s))=k$ for all $s$, since
$p(kb+sq)-q(ka+sp)=k(pb-qa)=k$.
\item \emph{Inversion}: $b\sigma_2-a\sigma_1=s$, so the substitution is unimodular; it is a
bijection of $\ZZ^2$ and $\{\sigma(k,s):s\in\ZZ\}$ is the full solution set of $pm-qn=k$.
\item \emph{Coprimality transfer}: $\gcd(\sigma_1,\sigma_2)=1\iff\gcd(k,s)=1$, both
directions following from the two B\'ezout expressions supplied by (1) and (2).
\item \emph{Parity}: $\sigma_1+\sigma_2=k(a+b)+s(p+q)$, an explicit affine function of $s$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Item (3) is the heart of the matter: the arithmetic of the \emph{node} is converted into the
arithmetic of the \emph{(charge, parameter)} pair.

\begin{proposition}[Ordering past a bound]\label{prop:bound}
Suppose $0<p<q$ and set $S_0(k,a,b)=|ka|+|kb|+|k(b-a)|+1$. Then for every integer $s\ge S_0$
the pair $\sigma(k,s)$ satisfies $0<\sigma_2<\sigma_1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma_2=ka+sp\ge-|ka|+s\ge1$ using $p\ge1$; and $\sigma_1-\sigma_2=k(b-a)+s(q-p)\ge
-|k(b-a)|+s\ge1$ using $q-p\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realisation theorem]\label{thm:realise}
Let $0<p<q$ be coprime and let $k\ne0$ be an integer which, in case $p+q$ is even, is odd.
Then for every bound $M$ there is a Euclid seed $(m,n)$ with $m>M$ and $\chg{p/q}(m,n)=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $a,b$ with $pb-qa=1$ and consider $\sigma(k,s)$ for large $s$. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:bound} the ordering condition holds for $s\ge S_0$; by
Proposition~\ref{prop:sigma}(3) primitivity is equivalent to $\gcd(k,s)=1$. For parity,
$\sigma_1+\sigma_2=k(a+b)+s(p+q)$. If $p+q$ is even then $p,q$ are both odd, so $pb-qa=1$
forces $b-a$ odd and hence $a+b$ odd; with $k$ odd the sum is odd $+$ even $=$ odd,
identically in $s$. If $p+q$ is odd, the requirement is the single congruence
$s\equiv\epsilon\pmod2$ with $\epsilon\equiv1-k(a+b)$; if $\epsilon$ is even then $k(a+b)$ is
odd, so $k$ is odd.

It remains to produce arbitrarily large $s$ meeting the active congruence and coprime to
$k$. If no congruence is active, or $\epsilon$ is odd, take $s=1+2j|k|$: it is odd and
$s\equiv1\pmod{|k|}$, so $\gcd(s,k)=1$. If $\epsilon$ is even then $k$ is odd and
$s=2+2j|k|$ is even with $\gcd(s,|k|)=\gcd(2,|k|)=1$. Letting $j\to\infty$ makes
$\sigma_1=kb+sq$ exceed any prescribed bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Every admissible ray is infinite]\label{cor:infinite}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:realise}, the set of Euclid seeds of charge $k$ at
$p/q$ is infinite.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The exact fan]\label{thm:fan}
For coprime $0<p<q$,
\[
\{\chg{p/q}(m,n):(m,n)\text{ a Euclid seed}\}=
\begin{cases}
\ZZ, & p+q\text{ odd},\\
\{k\in\ZZ:k\text{ odd}\}, & p+q\text{ even}.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $p+q$ is odd: charge $0$ is realised by $(q,p)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:axisparity}) and every
$k\ne0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:realise}. If $p+q$ is even then $p$ and $q$ are both odd, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} confines charges to the odd integers, all of which
Theorem~\ref{thm:realise} realises.
\end{proof}

This is a complete description of every fan: two-sided at an interior rational, spacing
$1/q$ in the hypercycle parameter, with exactly half the rays extinguished precisely when
$p$ and $q$ are both odd.

\section{The visibility law: which fans the eye sees}\label{sec:vis}

\begin{theorem}[Separation at fixed height]\label{thm:sep}
Two points at the same height $y=1/m$ whose charges at $p/q$ differ by $d$ are separated in
real part by exactly $|d|/(qm)=|d|\,y/q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\chg{p/q}(m,n)-\chg{p/q}(m,n')=-q(n-n')$, so $|n-n'|=|d|/q$, and the difference in real
parts is $|n-n'|/m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Adjacent rays ($d=1$) of the fan at $p/q$ are exactly $y/q$ apart at plot height $y$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution criterion]\label{thm:res}
For $y>0$ and resolution $\varepsilon>0$, the fan at $p/q$ has adjacent rays at least
$\varepsilon$ apart at height $y$ if and only if $q\le y/\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}
For $Q\ge1$ let $\mathcal F(Q)$ be the set of pairs $(p,q)$ with $1\le p\le q\le Q$ and
$\gcd(p,q)=1$: the star centres in $(0,1]$ of denominator at most $Q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Farey count]\label{thm:farey}
$\#\mathcal F(Q)=\sum_{q=1}^{Q}\varphi(q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition by denominator; for each $q$ the admissible numerators are the integers in $[1,q]$
coprime to $q$, of which there are $\varphi(q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Visibility law]\label{thm:visibility}
At plot height $y$ and resolution $\varepsilon$, put $Q=\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$. Every
fan centred at a point of $\mathcal F(Q)$ is resolved, no other centre in $(0,1]$ is, and
the number of resolved centres is $\sum_{q\le Q}\varphi(q)$: the Farey fractions of level
$Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
At $y=1/2$ and $\varepsilon=1/10$ one has $Q=5$ and $\#\mathcal F(5)=1+1+2+2+4=10$: the
centres $1/1,1/2,1/3,2/3,1/4,3/4,1/5,2/5,3/5,4/5$, together with the boundary centre $0$.
This is precisely the set of fans a plot at that scale displays---including those at $0.2$
and $0.333\ldots$, and excluding, say, $2/7$.
\end{example}

\section{Diophantine content: charges measure approximation}

\begin{theorem}[Approximation dictionary]\label{thm:dict}
For $q,m\ge1$,
\[
\frac nm-\frac pq=\frac{-\chg{p/q}(m,n)}{q\,m},
\]
and consequently, for any $K\ge0$,
$\bigl|n/m-p/q\bigr|\le K/(qm)\iff\bigl|\chg{p/q}(m,n)\bigr|\le K$.
\end{theorem}

So the rays of a fan are the levels of approximation quality: a node lies on a low ray at
$p/q$ exactly when $p/q$ approximates its slope well.

\begin{theorem}[Farey's theorem]\label{thm:fareythm}
Let $p/q$ and $n/m$ satisfy $q,m>0$ and $qn-pm=1$. Then no fraction $r/s$ with $0<s<q+m$
satisfies $p/q<r/s<n/m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $p/q<r/s<n/m$ then $rq-ps\ge1$ and $ns-rm\ge1$. Multiplying the first by $m$, the second
by $q$, and adding gives $s(qn-pm)=s\ge m+q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:mediant}
Under the same hypotheses, $p/q<(p+n)/(q+m)<n/m$, so the bound $q+m$ is attained by the
mediant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(p+n)/(q+m)-p/q=1/(q(q+m))>0$ and $n/m-(p+n)/(q+m)=1/(m(q+m))>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Innermost rays]\label{cor:innermost}
A Euclid seed of charge $-1$ at $p/q$ satisfies $qn-pm=1$, so its slope is a Farey neighbour
of $p/q$: nothing of denominator smaller than $q+m$ lies strictly between them, and the
slope differs from $p/q$ by exactly $1/(qm)$. The innermost spokes of a fan consist of best
rational approximations to its centre.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Two principal stars]\label{thm:two}
Let $(m,n)$ be a Euclid seed with $m\ge2$. There are distinct pairs $(p,q)\ne(p',q')$ with
$0<q<m$, $0<q'<m$ and $\chg{p/q}(m,n)=-1$, $\chg{p'/q'}(m,n)=+1$. Every node of the tree is
therefore an innermost node of at least two fans, of denominators smaller than its own.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $\gcd(n,m)=1$, $n$ is invertible modulo $m$; let $q\in(0,m)$ represent $n^{-1}$, so
$qn\equiv1\pmod m$. Choose $p$ with $qn-pm=1$, i.e.\ $\chg{p/q}(m,n)=-1$. Set $q'=m-q\in(0,m)$;
then $q'n\equiv-1\pmod m$, so there is $p'$ with $q'n-p'm=-1$, i.e.\ $\chg{p'/q'}(m,n)=+1$.
The pairs are distinct because the charge of $(m,n)$ at them differs.
\end{proof}

\section{How densely a ray is populated: a totient law}

Fix $p/q$ with $p$ and $q$ both odd (the fans at $1/3$, $1/5$, $3/5$, \ldots) and an odd
charge $k$; write $K=|k|$.

\begin{theorem}[Coprimality is the only obstruction]\label{thm:onlycop}
With $pb-qa=1$, for every integer $s\ge S_0(k,a,b)$ the pair $\sigma(k,s)$ is a Euclid seed
if and only if $\gcd(K,s)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:bound} gives $0<\sigma_2<\sigma_1$ and
Proposition~\ref{prop:sigma}(3) turns primitivity into $\gcd(k,s)=1$. For parity, $p,q$ odd
and $pb-qa=1$ force $a+b$ odd, so $\sigma_1+\sigma_2=k(a+b)+s(p+q)$ is odd plus even, hence
odd, identically in $s$. Thus the parity condition is automatic.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Window count]\label{thm:window}
For all $K\ge1$ and $N\ge0$,
$\#\{s\in[N,N+2K):\gcd(K,s)=1\}=2\varphi(K)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split the window into $[N,N+K)$ and $[N+K,N+2K)$, each a complete residue system modulo $K$
and hence each containing exactly $\varphi(K)$ integers coprime to $K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity split]\label{thm:split}
For all $K,N$,
\[
\#\{s\in[N,N+2K):\gcd(K,s)=1,\ s\text{ odd}\}=\varphi(2K),\qquad
\#\{\ldots,\ s\text{ even}\}=2\varphi(K)-\varphi(2K).
\]
For odd $K$ both classes contain exactly $\varphi(K)$ parameters, since
$\varphi(2K)=\varphi(K)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Coprimality to $2K$ is equivalent to coprimality to $K$ together with oddness, and a window
of length $2K$ contains exactly $\varphi(2K)$ integers coprime to $2K$. Subtract for the
even class.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Totient density law for a ray]\label{thm:density}
Let $p,q$ be odd with $0<p<q$ coprime, let $k$ be an odd charge and $K=|k|$. For every
$N\ge S_0(k,a,b)$, the number of Berggren nodes on the ray of charge $k$ at $p/q$ whose
unimodular parameter lies in $[N,N+2K)$ is exactly $2\varphi(K)=2\varphi(2K)$. Hence the ray
is infinite but of arithmetic density $\varphi(K)/K$ in the parameter.
\end{theorem}

The spoke of charge $1$ is completely full; the spoke of charge $3$ is two-thirds full; the
spoke of charge $15$ has density $8/15$ and is visibly dotted. Rays of highly composite
charge are the faint ones. Since $m=kb+sq$ is linear in the parameter, this is also a
density statement in the seed size: the number of nodes on the ray with $m\le M$ is
asymptotically $(\varphi(K)/K)(M/q)$.

\section{Transport: the tree permutes the fans}

\begin{theorem}[Star covariance]\label{thm:cov}
For all integers $p,q,m,n$,
\[
\chg{p/q}\bigl(B_1(m,n)\bigr)=\chg{(2p-q)/p}(m,n),\quad
\chg{p/q}\bigl(B_2(m,n)\bigr)=\chg{(2p-q)/(-p)}(m,n),\quad
\chg{p/q}\bigl(B_3(m,n)\bigr)=\chg{p/(q-2p)}(m,n),
\]
where $\chg{P/Q}$ denotes the bilinear form $Pm-Qn$ even when $(P,Q)$ is not normalised.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p(2m-n)-qm=(2p-q)m-pn$; $p(2m+n)-qm=(2p-q)m+pn$; $p(m+2n)-qn=pm-(q-2p)n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Transport action]
On the star parameter $v=(p,q)\in\ZZ^2$ set
\[
T_1(p,q)=(2p-q,\ p),\qquad T_2(p,q)=(2p-q,\ -p),\qquad T_3(p,q)=(p,\ q-2p),
\]
extended to words by composition. Theorem~\ref{thm:cov} says: the charge of a moved node at
a fan equals the charge of the original node at the transported fan.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Each $T_i$ has determinant $\pm1$, so transport preserves primitivity of $(p,q)$ and acts on
rational ideal points in lowest terms.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Parity invariant]\label{thm:transparity}
For each $i$ and every $v=(p,q)$, the parity of the coordinate sum of $T_i(v)$ equals that
of $v$; hence the same holds for every word.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(2p-q)+p=3p-q\equiv p+q$; $(2p-q)-p=p-q\equiv p+q$; $p+(q-2p)=q-p\equiv p+q$, all modulo
$2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Permanent asymmetry]\label{cor:asym}
No word of transports carries $(0,1)$ to $(1,1)$ or back: the sums $1$ and $2$ have
different parities. The $0$-fan (all charges) and the $1$-fan (odd charges only) lie in
different transport classes, so their visual asymmetry is intrinsic and not an artefact of
the choice of root.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:transparity} also explains the dichotomy of Theorem~\ref{thm:fan}: the two
classes of fan are exactly the two parity classes of the transport action, which can never
be mixed.

\begin{theorem}[The ladder]\label{thm:ladder}
Let $L_k=(k,k+1)$. Then $T_1(L_{k+1})=L_k$, hence $T_1^{\,k}(L_k)=L_0=(0,1)$: for every
$k\ge1$ the fan at $k/(k+1)$ is the transport of the $0$-fan by the word $B_1^{\,k}$. Its
parameter sum $2k+1$ is odd, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:fan} the fan is full, and by
Corollary~\ref{cor:asym} no word carries the $1$-fan to it.
\end{theorem}

So the fans at $1/2,2/3,3/4,4/5,\ldots$ marching towards the ideal point $1$ are all copies
of the fan at $0$. The picture is far more self-similar than it looks.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Charge and hypercycle level).} Given a seed $(m,n)$ and a centre $p/q$
in lowest terms, return $\chi=pm-qn$, the ray parameter $\chi/q$, the hyperbolic width
$\arsinh(|\chi|/q)$ and the approximation error $-\chi/(qm)$. Constant time.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Ray enumeration).} Compute $a,b$ with $pb-qa=1$ by the extended
Euclidean algorithm; for $s\ge S_0=|ka|+|kb|+|k(b-a)|+1$ form $(m,n)=(kb+sq,ka+sp)$ and
retain the Euclid seeds---equivalently, when $p,q,k$ are all odd, exactly those with
$\gcd(|k|,s)=1$. Cost $O(M/q)$ for seeds of size at most $M$, plus one extended gcd; a sieve
of the parameter window makes the coprimality test amortised $O(1)$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Fan census).} Enumerate all Euclid seeds with $m\le M$, compute their
charges at $p/q$, and tabulate the realised charges and spoke densities. Cost $O(M^2)$
naively.

\textbf{Algorithm D (Visibility list).} Given $y$ and $\varepsilon$, set
$Q=\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$ and output all $p/q$ with $1\le p\le q\le Q$ and
$\gcd(p,q)=1$, together with the count $\sum_{q\le Q}\varphi(q)$. Cost $O(Q^2)$, or
$O(Q\log\log Q)$ for the count alone.

\textbf{Algorithm E (Transport walk).} Apply $T_1,T_2,T_3$ letter by letter to a star
parameter, verifying that the parity of $p+q$ and the coprimality persist. Cost $O(|w|)$.

\section{Numerical evidence}

Exhaustive enumeration of seeds with $m\le400$, recording all charges of absolute value at
most $8$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
$p/q$ & $p+q$ & realised charges with $|\chi|\le8$\\
\midrule
$0/1$ & odd & $-8,\ldots,-1$\\
$1/1$ & even & $1,3,5,7$\\
$1/2$ & odd & $-8,\ldots,8$ (including $0$)\\
$1/3$ & even & $\pm1,\pm3,\pm5,\pm7$\\
$1/5$ & even & $\pm1,\pm3,\pm5,\pm7$\\
$2/5$ & odd & $-8,\ldots,8$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:fan} predicts. Counting seeds with $m\le20000$ on the ray of
charge $k$ at $p/q$, normalised by the number $M/q$ of admissible $m$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lllll}
\toprule
$p/q$ & $k$ & count & count$/(M/q)$ & $\varphi(K)/K$\\
\midrule
$1/3$ & $1$ & $6666$ & $0.9999$ & $1$\\
$1/3$ & $3$ & $4444$ & $0.6666$ & $0.6667$\\
$1/5$ & $5$ & $3200$ & $0.8000$ & $0.8$\\
$1/2$ & $3$ & $3333$ & $0.3333$ & $0.6667$\\
$1/2$ & $6$ & $3333$ & $0.3333$ & $0.3333$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The odd/odd rows realise the density $\varphi(K)/K$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:density} exactly.
The rows with $p+q$ odd lose a further factor $2$ when $K$ is odd---precisely the parity
split of Theorem~\ref{thm:split}, since there the parity of the node is not automatic and
only one residue class of the parameter survives.

\section{Discussion}

The results assemble into one statement: the star map produced by embedding the Berggren
tree into the hyperbolic plane is a picture of the rational numbers, and every visible
feature of it is exactly computable. Where the rays are: at every rational $p/q$, one ray
per integer charge. What a ray is: a hypercycle at distance $\arsinh(|\chi|/q)$ from the
geodesic over $p/q$. Which rays exist: all of them when $p+q$ is odd, exactly the odd ones
when $p+q$ is even; the axis holds at most the single node $(q,p)$. Which fans one sees:
those with $q\le y/\varepsilon$, the Farey fractions of level $\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$.
What a low ray means: good Diophantine approximation, with the innermost rays consisting of
Farey neighbours. How thick a ray is: arithmetic density $\varphi(|k|)/|k|$. How the fans
relate: by an integral linear transport conserving the charge and the parity of $p+q$.

Two structural lessons deserve emphasis. First, \emph{unimodularity is the engine}: nearly
every statement is proved by substituting $(m,n)=(kb+sq,ka+sp)$ and observing that a
determinant-one substitution transports arithmetic faithfully---coprimality of the node
becomes coprimality of $(k,s)$, parity becomes an affine congruence in $s$, and the ordering
$0<n<m$ becomes a linear inequality. Second, \emph{geometry becomes arithmetic, exactly}: the
adjacent-ray gap is $y/q$ with no error term, so ``which fans does the plot resolve'' has the
literal answer ``the Farey fractions of level $\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$'', and nothing
hyperbolic survives in the final statement.

Finally, the parity invariant is a two-valued conserved quantity of a free ternary tree
acting on the projective line over $\QQ$, partitioning the fans into two eternally separated
families. That the two most conspicuous features of the picture sit on opposite sides of
this partition is the reason the picture looks lopsided, and no rerooting can make it
symmetric.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Farey visibility asymptotic.} With $Q=\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$, the number of
resolved fans is $\Phi(Q)=\sum_{q\le Q}\varphi(q)$, expected to satisfy
$\Phi(Q)=3Q^2/\pi^2+O(Q\log Q)$. The reduction of the geometric visibility question to
$\Phi(Q)$ is exact, so what remains is purely analytic; the payoff is the first quantitative
prediction about the picture (doubling the resolution quadruples the number of visible
fans). Falsifiable by any enumeration whose ratio $\Phi(Q)/Q^2$ stabilises away from
$3/\pi^2=0.30396\ldots$

\textbf{Exact node count on a ray.} On the ray of odd charge $k$ at an odd/odd rational, the
number of nodes with $m\le M$ should be $(\varphi(|k|)/|k|)(M/q)+O(\sigma_0(|k|)+q)$. The
exact periodic count is known; the missing ingredient is the passage from the parameter $s$
to the seed size through $m=kb+sq$.

\textbf{The even-sum fans.} The totient law was proved for $p,q$ both odd, where the parity
condition is automatic. When $p+q$ is odd the parity condition selects one residue class of
$s$ modulo $2$; the exact four-case density law (parity of $p+q$ against parity of $K$)
remains to be formulated and proved.

\textbf{Angular equidistribution.} Do the nodes with hypotenuse in $[N,2N]$ equidistribute
over the corresponding hyperbolic annulus? The star structure says the answer is far from a
smooth density at any finite resolution.

\textbf{Modular interpretation of transport.} The transports generate a subgroup of
$\mathrm{GL}_2(\ZZ)$ acting on star parameters. Identifying that group and its orbit
structure---with the parity of $p+q$ as invariant and the ladder $k/(k+1)$ as one explicit
orbit---would place the fan system inside modular-curve geometry, where the parity dichotomy
should appear as a cusp-width phenomenon.

\textbf{Higher stars.} Is there a canonical second-order structure organising the star
centres themselves? The ladder theorem, collapsing infinitely many fans onto the $0$-fan, is
a first hint that the centres inherit a tree structure of their own.

\appendix

